ty exhaustion hypothesis. Doubling worker count from 8 to 16 yielded only 6% queue reduction over ten minutes. Under normal circumstances, doubling concurrency should approximately halve queue depth (assuming linear scaling). The minimal effect proves the bottleneck was not worker availability but external rate limiting. Workers could be instantiated, but they would immediately hit the rate limit on dependency B. This is equivalent to hiring more cashiers when the bank's vault can only dispense cash at a fixed rate—more workers cannot bypass the upstream constraint. The evidence strongly indicates that the original eight workers were already sufficient to saturate dependency B's rate limit.\n\nRetry Policy Assessment:\nThe retry policy proved to be iatrogenic—a treatment worse than the disease. E08 provides definitive evidence: temporarily disabling retries caused queue depth to fall 38% in just six minutes. This six-minute improvement from a single policy change demonstrates that retries were the primary load multiplier. The tradeoff was increased direct failure rate, implying that customers experienced task failures rather than delayed success. However, the incident was caused not by transient failures but by rate limiting, which is not resolved by retries. Retrying a 429 response after exponential backoff is mathematically futile if the rate limit itself has not changed. The configuration lacked awareness of rate-limit semantics; HTTP 429 should trigger a different strategy than transient 5xx errors. The exponential backoff was reasonable for transient failures but created queuing chaos under rate-limiting conditions.\n\nResolution and Validation:\nE09 confirms the diagnosis and validates the root cause. When dependency B's rate limits were raised, HTTP 429 responses fell below 1% within minutes, and queue depth returned below 150 within eleven minutes. This is the decisive evidence: the minute the upstream constraint was relaxed, the incident resolved instantly. No code changes, no scaling, no retry adjustments—only an upstream rate limit increase restored normalcy. This resolution proves beyond doubt that rate limiting was the causal factor.\n\nInternal Constraints Ruled Out:\nE10 explicitly states that no deploy, schema change, host restart, network route change, or credential rotation occurred between 08:00-10:00. This rules out many plausible internal causes: code regression, configuration drift, routing changes, or authentication failures. The incident cannot be attributed to internal system change.\n\nAlternative Hypotheses Rejected:\nAlternative 1: System Capacity Exhaustion was rejected because E04 shows CPU 43%, memory 51%, disk 22% (all below saturation), and E07 shows that doubling workers had minimal effect. If the bottleneck were local, scaling would help proportionally.\n\nAlternative 2: Network Degradation was rejected because E10 rules out network route changes, E05 explicitly identifies HTTP 429 responses (not packet loss), and network issues would affect bidirectional traffic symmetrically, not just outbound dependency B requests.\n\nAlternative 3: Pathological Task Distribution was rejected because task latency spike (E03) correlates directly with dependency B 429s (E05), and E10 confirms no schema or task type changes occurred.\n\nUncertainties Remaining:\nWhile the root cause is clear, several unknowns persist: (1) whether the rate limit change on dependency B was intentional or an automated response to external demand; (2) the exact mechanism that triggered rate limiting at 09:15; (3) whether any customer-facing SLA breaches occurred during the retry disablement window; (4) historical rate limit utilization on dependency B to assess whether this was a capacity crisis or a policy change; (5) whether any requests were permanently dropped due to queue overflow.\n\nRecommendations:\nStaged remediation should prioritize: (1) adaptive retry policy with circuit breaker pattern to detect and respond to rate limits; (2) formal SLA and rate limit contract with dependency B with advance notice of changes; (3) real-time rate limit monitoring and alerting; (4) bulkhead isolation to prevent dependency B issues from cascading; (5) quarterly dependency capacity planning reviews; (6) implementation of jitter and exponential backoff caps to prevent retry storms.\n\nConclusion:\nThe incident was caused by dependency B rate limiting, amplified by an aggressive retry policy that lacked rate-limit awareness. Scaling workers could not overcome an external rate limit. The incident resolved instantly when the upstream constraint was relaxed, definitively validating the root cause diagnosis.",
